# Kotlin service style

- Time: inject `Clock`, use `OffsetDateTime.now(clock)`. Never call bare `OffsetDateTime.now()`.
- Timestamps on insert: do not set `createdAt` / `updatedAt` in code when the column has `DEFAULT now()`; let the DB fill them.
- Timestamps on update: set `updatedAt` via `Clock`.
- gRPC write handlers: return only what the client needs (e.g. created id), not the full aggregate.
- Shared contracts: put `.proto` / API in a sibling `*-api` module; reusable infra (db-queue) in `common`.
- Transactions: declare `TransactionTemplate` in a shared `TransactionConfig`, not inside feature configs.
- db-queue: base in `common` — import `DbQueueConfiguration`; payloads implement `QueuePayload` with companion `QUEUE_NAME`; consumers extend `JsonQueueConsumer<T>` (type + queue name resolved from `T`); enqueue via `DbQueueEnqueueService`; config via `db-queue.properties`.
- Persistence: extend jOOQ DAOs in repositories; prefer DAO/`insert`/`update` over raw DSL when enough.
- Domain enums: UPPER_CASE Kotlin names; store lowercase DB values via converters.
- Keep configs thin: one concern per `@Configuration` class (`TimeConfig`, `TransactionConfig`, `DbQueueConfig`, …).
- Functions: block body with `return` only — no expression-body (`=`) functions.
- Avoid redundant `checkNotNull`; prefer `!!` / APIs that already guarantee a value (`fetchSingleInto`, etc.).
- Proto: domain messages/enums in their own files; service file keeps only the service + request/response.
- CI: PR checks run ktlint (`ktlintCheck`) and full `test` (Postgres + Liquibase for jOOQ codegen, Testcontainers for IT).
